
####################################################################################
   This is the example ML fit from dump descriptor files vs classical fit
####################################################################################

-- TO PERFORM THE CLASSICAL FIT --

   Set vacancy.ml_classic as the principle input file:
       >> rm -rf vacancy.ml ; ln -s vacancy.ml_classic vacancy.ml

   Run MiLaDy and write the output of the fit to OUT_CLASSIC:
       >> mpirun -np 2 milady_main.exe  > OUT_CLASSIC


-- TO PERFORM THE FIT AND WRITE DUMP DESCRIPTOR FILES --

   Set vacancy.ml_write_dump as the principle input file:
       >> rm -rf vacancy.ml ; ln -s vacancy.ml_write_dump vacancy.ml

   Run MiLaDy and write the output of the fit to OUT_WRITE:
       >> mpirun -np 2 milady_main.exe  > OUT_WRITE


-- TO PERFORM THE FIT BASED ON READING OF DUMP DESCRIPTOR FILES --

   Set vacancy.ml_read_dump as the principle input file:
       >> rm -rf vacancy.ml ; ln -s vacancy.ml_read_dump vacancy.ml

   Run MiLaDy and write the output of the fit to OUT_READ:
       >> mpirun -np 2 milady_main.exe  > OUT_READ


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


the output of fit (after the logo of MiLaDy) in files:
  - OUT_CLASSIC
  - OUT_WRITE
  - OUT_READ

should not differ more than within a numerical error.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The database files provided in this example do not represent a complete database.
Please, make sure that the fit works fine for the real dataset.
